{ "my_vector2": [1.5, 5.5, 4.5, 6.4], "price": 8.9 }
Then you can search the data using the knn query type.
GET my-index/_search
{
"size": 2,
"query": {
"knn": {
"my_vector2": {
"vector": [2, 3, 5, 6],
"k": 2
}
}
}
}
In this case, k is the number of neighbors you want the query to return, but you must also include
the size option. Otherwise, you get k results for each shard (and each segment) rather than k
results for the entire query. k-NN supports a maximum k value of 10,000.
If you mix the knn query with other clauses, you might receive fewer than k results. In this
example, the post_filter clause reduces the number of results from 2 to 1.
GET my-index/_search
{
"size": 2,
"query": {
"knn": {
